        "knowledge_point": "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions",
        "problem": "Consider the function $f(x) =3e^{x}-1.$ Given that $y = f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $(f^{-1})'(2).$",
        "worked_solution": "To determine $(f^{-1})'(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\left(f^{-1}\\right)'(2) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of $f^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation $f^{-1}(2) = x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ 3{e}^{x}-1 & =2 \\\\ 3{e}^{x} & =3 \\\\ {e}^{x} & =1 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f^{-1}(2) = 0.$\n\nNow, we calculate $f'(0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3{e}^{x}-1 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =3{e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(0) & =3{e}^{0} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(0) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{\u2032}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "screenshot": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/example-14722.png"
      }
    ],
    "feedback": [
      {
        "math_academy_id": "q-19201",
        "problem": "The following table gives the values of $f(x),{f}^{\u2032}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{\u2032}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{\u2032}(2)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{\u2032}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-1$ | $-6$ | $-3$ | $1$ |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $-2$ |",
        "worked_solution": "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function ${f}^{-1}(x)g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(x))}+{g}^{\u2032}(x){f}^{-1}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{\u2032}(2)=\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(2))}+{g}^{\u2032}(2){f}^{-1}(2).\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(2)=-1,$ $g(2)=-3,$ ${g}^{\u2032}(2)=1,$ and ${f}^{\u2032}(-1)=-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{\u2032}(2) & =\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(2))}+{g}^{\u2032}(2){f}^{-1}(2) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{{f}^{\u2032}(-1)}+1\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{-2}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
